  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Added:\n- A benchmark project measuring one binary COPY through this library against\n  the hand-written NpgsqlBinaryImporter loop it replaces, over a four-column\n  and a twelve-column row at 10,000 and 100,000 rows, and the two\n  WriteDataAsync overloads against each other. On the recorded baseline the\n  mapping costs 1.08x on the narrow row and 1.04x on the wide one at 100,000\n  rows, the async source costs 1.01x, and the extra allocation is a few\n  kilobytes per copy, a figure that does not grow with the row count. The\n  README now carries those numbers. The project ships in the repository, not\n  in the package, and nothing in the build or the release gates on it.\n\nChanged:\n- WriteDataAsync now throws InvalidOperationException when no column has been\n  mapped, instead of returning 0 and sending nothing. A builder with no Map*\n  call is a forgotten mapping, not a request for an empty copy, and the silent\n  no-op hid it. Callers that relied on the 0 have to guard the call themselves;\n  nothing else changes.\n- WriteDataAsync now decides what to do from the connection's full state rather\n  than from the two-valued ConnectionState. A closed connection is opened for\n  the copy and closed again, and a broken one is reconnected the same way, both\n  as before. A connection busy with another command or an open reader is now\n  rejected with an InvalidOperationException naming that state, instead of\n  failing from inside the copy with an error that named neither the connection\n  nor what it was doing.\n- The date and time Map* methods name their parameters builder and getter, like\n  every other Map* method. This is source-breaking only for a caller that passed\n  them by name as helper or propertyGetter.",
